The child bash that sub_agent and explore_agent get now refuses git redirection escapes when its parent is pinned to a worktree, using the parent's own check and the parent's exact refusal text. It also now runs inside that worktree. That closes the last layer of #977. Build, the full test suite, clippy and fmt all pass; the commit is 1e5a5e91 and it's pushed.

Can a pinned parent build a child at all? Yes. Spawn workers get a pinned bash_cwd (commands_spawn.rs:877 and :1028). Building their agent then adds sub_agent (agent_builder.rs:1266) and explore_agent (:1317), so this path is real and worth guarding.

What changed: both callers of sub_agent_child_tools now pass in the parent's pinned directory.
• **With a pin:** the child refuses the same `-C`, `--git-dir`, `--work-tree`, `GIT_DIR=` and `GIT_WORK_TREE=` cases the parent does, with byte-identical wording.
• **Without a pin (every ordinary session):** the check is never called and the child is built as before.

One change beyond the task's steps: I also made the child's bash run in the pinned directory. Before, it ran in the main checkout, so a plain git commit from a spawn worker's child already landed outside the worktree. Refusing git -C /outside while the default directory was already outside would have protected nothing.

Tests (in src/tools_child_bash_tests.rs, built through the real sub_agent_child_tools):
• **Refused:** three escape commands, for both kinds of child. Each refusal is compared with `assert_eq!` against the parent's message and against a pinned parent's real refusal. Each input is also checked to really trip the parent's detector.
• **Allowed:**
  • A pinned child runs git status and git log --oneline without refusal, and pwd -P shows the pinned directory.
  • An unpinned child does not refuse the escape commands. That covers ordinary sessions.
• **Positive control:** neutering the new check made exactly the refusal test fail. After restoring it everything passed, and the `NEUTERED` marker is gone.

Every BashTool::default in non-test code is now pinned and wrapped; the one other match is a test-only helper that reads the tool's name and never runs it.

I added a dated entry under src/tools.rs in ARCHITECTURE.md and rewrote the old "not checked here" comment in tools.rs. I posted the result on #977 and closed it: every item its body listed is now covered for the child, with detect_write_command coming through the read/plan guard the child already had.

This has the same reach as the parent's check: a bash command with an absolute path can still get out of the worktree. It keeps git in the worktree by default; it is not a sandbox.
